            <ArticleTitle>Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia associated with intramuscular myxomas: Mazabraud syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>280-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/0971-3026.161456</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The authors report a new case of Mazabraud syndrome in a 69-year-old woman complaining of pain in her right thigh. Plain radiographs demonstrated radiological findings consistent with polyostotic fibrous dysplasia of the right femur and tibia. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study showed soft tissue tumors located in the vastus intermedius muscle with typical signal features of intramuscular myxomas. Biopsy was not performed because of its benign nature. Symptomatic treatment was prescribed and all the lesions remained 1 year after the diagnosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
